@title Rotate edge proxy TLS certificates
@subtitle Runbook for the 18 edge proxies
@author Platform Security, Platform team

kpi Rotate when | 30 days left | CERT_EXPIRY_30D alert
kpi Hostnames | 12 | covered by each certificate
kpi Canary error limit | 0.1 percent | held for 20 minutes
kpi Escalate above | 2 percent | handshake errors

# When to rotate

Rotate a certificate when it has 30 days or fewer left. The CERT_EXPIRY_30D alert announces this. The certificates cover 12 hostnames.

# Before you start

- [ ] The change record is open.
- [ ] No deploy is running in #deploys.
- [ ] The internal CA is healthy.

# Rotation steps

!!! Never delete the old private key before step 6. It stays in place until the old certificate is revoked.

1. **Confirm expiry.**
-- a) Run `openssl x509 -enddate -noout` on the live certificate.
-- b) Compare the date with the alert.
2. **Request the new certificate.**
-- a) Run `certctl request --profile edge-prod`.
-- b) Check that the output lists all 12 hostnames.
3. **Stage it on the canary proxy only.**
-- a) Copy the new certificate to `/etc/edge/certs/next` on edge-canary-01.
-- b) Reload the proxy.
4. **Verify the canary.**
-- a) Run `openssl s_client -connect edge-canary-01:443` and confirm the new expiry date.
-- b) Handshake errors stay under 0.1 percent for 20 minutes.
5. **Roll out to the other 17 proxies** in batches of 3, waiting 5 minutes between batches.
6. **Revoke the old certificate** after 48 hours, and record it in the change record.

# Rollback

The previous certificate stays in `/etc/edge/certs/previous` for 7 days. To roll back, copy it back and reload the proxy.

# Escalation

- Page Platform Security if any proxy shows more than 2 percent handshake errors.
- Post in #platform-sec.
